AUDIO: 'Hi and welcome back to a new video. Today we want to experiment and investigate a myth that has been around as long as I can remember in the PC industry. At some point, if you\'re building a new PC, you will come to the decision of how to place the direction of your fans. Do you want to have cold'

[00:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'air intake on top or do you want to push out hot air outside of your case? And especially on German forums, I often read, "Hey, support the chimney effect." Most likely known as stack effect in the English community. It just push out the'

[00:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hot air from inside. But what about this? Is there even a stack effect or chimney effect? Is your PC a chimney? And does that even work? That\'s what we want to find out in'

[00:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'today\'s video. Thermal Grizzly Aeronaut is our new high-end thermal paste and the successor of Kryonaut. It\'s even better performing, it is much easier to apply, it is cheaper, and it\'s much more durable. That\'s where the name comes from. Especially if you\'re now maybe'

[00:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'considering to buy a new PC, then I would highly recommend that you\'re looking into this thermal paste. So let\'s continue with this video. Most of the time these type of recommendations to support the stack effect come from the common knowledge that hot air rises.'

[01:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'As an example, we are looking at this system that we built a while ago on my channel. It\'s custom water cooled, but that doesn\'t matter for the moment. We have a big radiator that\'s placed on top. You see the black tubing going there, and the black tubing is directing'

[01:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the hot water into the radiator. That would be exactly the same if you would use just a normal conventional AIO. And underneath you can see the three fans that are using basically the warm air inside the system and pushing it through'

[01:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the radiator, cooling down the water that is sitting in the radiator, and then just hot air coming out of the system on the top. Most people are absolutely aware that hot air rises, but that\'s not stack effect. Hot air rising'

[01:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is caused by the molecules just having more movement if they\'re warmer, and that\'s causing a different density, which causes the hot air to rise. But, that\'s natural convection. That\'s not stack effect. In contrast to natural'

[02:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'convection around an individual heat source, the stack effect describes a large-scale airflow through a vertical enclosure or channel or chimney. Warm air inside the enclosure has a lower density than the cooler surrounding air,'

[02:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and therefore creates a small pressure difference between the lower and upper openings. This pressure difference then causes colder air to enter at the bottom, while warm air leaves at the top. The effect becomes stronger if you'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'increase the height or the temperature difference. This means that natural convection is indeed the base for the stack effect, but you need an enclosure. You need need something big, like a big case or like a big chimney, to create this pressure difference inside,'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'otherwise you would not have the flow inside. If we go back to our example, this PC build, we can assume that our case is basically the enclosure, the chimney. It has a height of 55 cm, and we can assume an ambient temperature of'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '20° C. Maybe assume internal temperature of 40° C. This would leave us with a stack effect and a pressure difference of 0.4 Pa. Pascal as a unit is usually not that'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'easy to imagine, especially when we\'re talking about very little pressure, like 0.4 Pa. So, that\'s why we have an example of the water column. So, you just imagine you have this hard tube, it\'s 1 m long, and you fill it with the water, like 1 m of'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'water, then you would have a pressure of 0.1 bar on your finger on the bottom. To get the pressure of 0.4 Pa, you would have to fill this hard tube'

[03:30] [scene:talk]
